{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Interesting. The image was already built 6 minutes ago (by a previous attempt? Wait, the steering said the previous attempt built the image but didn't commit. But the image was built \"6 minutes ago\" — meaning about 09:07? Now let me check the current time and the previous attempts.\n\nHmm, actually the image history shows a build from about 20-46 minutes ago. The \"6 minutes ago\" top layer. And there's the fork checkout at /home/tom/.cache/flashnext/vllm-fork. The current worktree was created at 09:13 (from the ls timestamps). Interesting point: TRITON_PIN=triton==3.8.0+git4cff872c.rocm7.14.0 — wait, the wheel cache has triton-3.6.0+rocm7.14.0. Hmm, but the triton installed in the image is 3.8.0+git4cff872c.rocm7.14.0? That's from the amd index — \"the triton wheel that matches the torch set in the same index\". Interesting: the cache triton 3.6.0 didn't match, so they resolved the actual triton that torch 2.13.0 requires? Actually, IMPORTS says the fallback is the pytorch-triton-rocm wheel that torch declares. Hmm, but the history shows TRITON_PIN=triton==3.8.0+git4cff872c.rocm7.14.0 installed from the index. Wait — but IMPORTS says the fp8 upcast gate is keyed to triton < 3.8! If triton is 3.8.0, then FORCE_FP8_DOT_UPCAST won't be enabled… hmm. Actually, is 3.8.0+git4cff872c < 3.8? No. In PEP 440, \"3.8.0+git...\" == 3.8.0. Hmm, but that's what the previous attempt did, and the image built fine. The goal says \"record the exact resolved triton version.\" So maybe the actual resolution result was 3.8.0+git.... Wait, but the wheel cache advisory said the pre-downloaded triton is 3.6.0. Perhaps 3.6.0 is what the index provides as triton-3.6.0+rocm7.14.0. Hmm.\n\nActually wait — the previous build installed `pip install --index-url ${ROCM_WHL} torch==2.13.0+rocm7.14.0 torchvision==... triton==3.8.0+git4cff872c.rocm7.14.0 rocm-sdk-devel==7.14.0 device-pin-group...`. So in the previous attempt, the resolved triton was identified as 3.8.0+git4cff872c.rocm7.14.0 (probably what the torch wheel declares as a dependency on pytorch-triton-rocm). The 3.6.0 triton wheel in the cache might be something else (e.g. a base triton package, not pytorch-triton-rocm).\n\nHmm, but the fp8 gate: \"gate on_gfx1151() and triton < 3.8\". If triton is 3.8.0, then the upcast gate would be disabled? That's a concern about the fork, but the container task just says to record the resolved triton. Actually, wait — perhaps what the torch 2.13.0 wheel requires is exactly \"pytorch-triton-rocm==3.8.0+git4cff872c\", hmm. Actually, the wheel cache had \"triton-3.6.0+rocm7.14.0\" — this doesn't match \"pytorch-triton-rocm\". Perhaps on the vendor index, triton 3.6.0+rocm7.14.0 exists, and also pytorch-triton-rocm==3.8.0+git4cff872c.rocm7.14.0. The gate keys on triton<3.8… but smoke tests the \"admission\" of the patched oracle — if the gate is disabled, the admission result might still pass via the fork patch (the FN_FP8_MOE default is on). Not my problem tonight — per the goal my task is to record the resolved version.\n\nWait, actually — is there a chance I should prefer triton 3.6.0 (the wheel in the cache)? The goal says \"install the triton wheel that matches [the torch set] from the vendor stable index… (record the exact resolved triton version).\" So: resolve whatever the index/torch resolves to. The previous attempt resolved it to 3.8.0+git4cff872c. If I change the pin, I invalidate the layer cache (pip install would rerun). To keep the build fast via the layer cache, I should reproduce the exact Containerfile so podman can use the cached layers. In practice, podman's layer cache keys on instruction contents + layer contents. If the Containerfile is identical (down to instruction text and order), the layer cache hits and the build finishes in seconds.\n\nCritical decision: reconstruct the previous Containerfile exactly (to hit the layer cache), then commit it. podman history gives me all the RUN commands. Reconstruct the Containerfile from history:\n\n1. FROM docker.io/library/ubuntu:24.04\n2. ARG ROCM_WHL TRITON_PIN TORCH_PIN TORCHVISION_PIN ROCM_DEVEL_PIN DEVICE_PINS (from the final ARG line: \"|8 ... ARG DEVICE_PINS ROCM_DEVEL_PIN ROCM_WHL TORCHVISION_PIN TORCH_PIN TRITON_PIN\" — 6 ARGs, 8 build args including 2 defaults? The |8 means 8 variables in the context. Hmm, the last executed ARG line shows 6 ARGs. Default values are probably ROCM_WHL=https://repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-multi-arch/, TORCH_PIN=torch==2.13.0+rocm7.14.0, etc.)\n3. ENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive\n4. RUN apt-get install python3.12 ... git ca-certificates curl build-essential cmake ninja-build ccache cargo rustc libnuma-dev\n5. ENV VIRTUAL_ENV=/opt/venv PATH=... PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=0 PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1 CCACHE_DIR=/root/.ccache HOME=/root\n6. RUN python3.12 -m venv /opt/venv\n7. RUN pip install --index-url ${ROCM_WHL} --extra-index-url https://pypi.org/simple setuptools wheel packaging \"setuptools-scm>=8.0\" setuptools-rust\n8. RUN pip install --index-url ${ROCM_WHL} torch torchvision triton rocm-sdk-devel device-pin-group\n9. ARG ... VLLM_REPO VLLM_BRANCH\n10. ENV VLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=rocm PYTORCH_ROCM_ARCH=gfx1151\n11. RUN git clone... (with an if guard) → /opt/flashnext-fork-commit\n12. RUN pip install -e /opt/vllm --no-build-isolation\n13. COPY container/rootfs/ → site-packages (dir:e37e34... 4.61kB)\n14. RUN python3 → /opt/flashnext/build-info.json (uses heredoc)\n15. RUN cat /opt/flashnext/build-info.json\n16. WORKDIR /opt/vllm\n17. CMD [\"bash\"]\n\nNote: the layer cache also keys on the contents of the COPY directory (dir:e37e...). I need container/rootfs/ with the same contents as last time. That's the fn_ instrument modules per THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES: fn_synctrace.py, fn_expert_union.py, fn_offload_batch.py, with upstream notices. These are \"adapted copies under container/rootfs/\". Do they exist somewhere? Perhaps in /home/tom/