Multiple ARGs on separate lines are collapsed? The history shows two \"ARG a b c...\" entries, one with 8 variables and one with 10. Hmm — maybe the Containerfile has two ARG instructions: `ARG DEVICE_PINS ROCM_DEVEL_PIN ROCM_WHL TORCHVISION_PIN TORCH_PIN TRITON_PIN` (this is invalid — ARG takes one name per line!). Actually, ARG accepts only one name. \"ARG DEVICE_PINS ROCM_DEVEL_PIN\" would be invalid… unless buildah tolerates it? Hmm, actually, multiple names on one ARG line: `ARG name1 name2` — is this invalid per the spec, or does Docker treat the remainder as default value? Does Docker allow `ARG name=default`, and multiple ARGs must be on separate lines? Let me think: \"ARG <name>[=<default value>]\" — one per instruction. But the history might consolidate consecutive ARG instructions? No, the history shows each RUN as a separate entry, but consecutive ARGs should also show separately… the bottom shows \"ARG ROCM_WHL\" separately from another consolidated line. Confusing — the earlier failed builds show \"ARG ROCM_WHL\" as its own entry (59 minutes ago, multiple attempts). The last successful build has an 8-variable ARG entry and a 10-variable ARG entry. If each ARG instruction appears separately, there should be 8 entries. So buildah might consolidate consecutive ARG instructions into one history entry. Hmm, but then why do the earlier attempts show \"ARG ROCM_WHL\" alone… those were probably single-line ARGs in the earlier draft.\n\nThe safest approach for cache hits: write a Containerfile with a single ARG instruction enumerating all 8 names (with defaults), followed by a second ARG instruction adding VLLM_REPO VLLM_BRANCH. buildah seems to accept multi-name ARG lines (since it built). Indeed, if it built with an 8-name ARG line, reproducing that exact line would hit.\n\nBut exact reconstruction is risky. The |8 vs |10 prefixes: the build executed with 8 variables defined for the first RUN (no VLLM_REPO/VLLM_BRANCH) and 10 for the clone. So in the Containerfile, the first ARG instruction declares 6 names, then the RUN uses them, then a second ARG adds VLLM_REPO VLLM_BRANCH (making it 8→10… wait 8+2=10). Hmm: |8 means 8 ARGs defined: 6 from the first ARG + …hmm 6 names. Unless the first ARG instruction has 8 names? The history entry \"|8 DEVICE_PINS=... TRITON_PIN=... /bin/sh -c pip install --index-url ${ROCM_WHL} ${TORCH_PIN}...\" shows 8 variables defined: DEVICE_PINS, ROCM_DEVEL_PIN, ROCM_WHL, TORCHVISION_PIN, TORCH_PIN, TRITON_PIN = 6. Plus 2 more not shown in the truncated output? The CREATED BY string might be truncated. Similarly, the |10 entries show the same 6 + VLLM_BRANCH, VLLM_REPO = 8. Hmm, the ARG entry after \"|10\" is \"ARG DEVICE_PINS ROCM_DEVEL_PIN ROCM_WHL TORCHVISION_PIN TORCH_PIN TRITON_PIN VLLM_BRANCH VLLM_REPO\" = 8 names.\n\nThe truncated output might be hiding variables. The safest approach: reproduce exactly the instruction text shown, including the multi-name ARG lines. Since the |N count includes all ARGs declared up to that point, the first group has 8 declared variables (6 shown + 2 truncated?). Hmm, risky. Or more simply: the history strings aren't truncated in the middle (they were displayed in full). Let me get the full JSON-format history to avoid truncation: podman history --format json.\n\nActually, an alternative approach: the exact cache hit isn't strictly necessary — just cheap. The expensive layers depend on the identity of instruction text + parent. If my reconstruction misses, the whole build is redone (64 minutes). High stakes. Let me use --format json to get the exact strings.\n\nAlso — I might find the previous Containerfile on disk! The previous attempt wrote container/ to a worktree. After failure, the driver may have reset the worktree, but the files might be in git objects? git status is clean, nothing was committed. Perhaps the previous attempt ran in the same worktree and the harness wiped it. Check /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/efca32942b