{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Now I've grasped the entire shape. The `|8` prefix indicates 8 ARG variables — 6 are displayed + … hmm, count of what's shown: DEVICE_PINS, ROCM_DEVEL_PIN, ROCM_WHL, TORCHVISION_PIN, TORCH_PIN, TRITON_PIN = 6. Plus maybe 2 hidden ones? |8 means there are 8 variables in the ARG context. Maybe there are 2 additional ARGs that have default values but don't appear in this list… actually, wait, maybe they were shown truncated? No, the string is complete. Hmm: maybe there are 2 additional ARGs declared between \"ARG DEVICE_PINS...\" and the pip install? The history shows the first ARG entry after ENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND: \"ARG DEVICE_PINS ROCM_DEVEL_PIN ROCM_WHL TORCHVISION_PIN TORCH_PIN TRITON_PIN\" — one multi-name ARG instruction that enumerates 6 names. buildah seems to have consolidated multiple ARG lines into one? And the \"|8\" count: 6 + base 2? Actually, |N counts the number of defined build arguments including those inherited from the base image (like RELEASE, LAUNCHPAD_BUILD_ARCH, etc.)? The ubuntu image has ARG RELEASE, ARG LAUNCHPAD_BUILD_ARCH — those are declared in the base but not in scope… hmm, they're in the base config though. ARGs in the base image don't carry over. Hmm, but the \"6 shown + 2\" might be something like that.\n\nAnyway — the critical issue for cache hits is: does buildah's cache key depend on the exact instruction text, or on the resolved values? For RUN, buildah uses the command string + environment. The command string in history is what got executed with ${VAR} already expanded? No — history shows the expanded values (expanded by buildah into |N VAR=value). The actual stored command is `pip install --index-url ${ROCM_WHL} ...` and buildah records the ARG values as metadata. For cache matching, buildah hashes instruction + input layer. If the instruction text differs, cache misses.\n\nRather than reverse-engineering buildah's cache keys with an exact text reproduction, let's do a live test: write my own Containerfile reconstruction, run `podman build`, and see if layers get reused. If cache misses occur in the early layers, we can abort before expensive steps (apt takes a few minutes; we can kill it and adapt). Better yet: reconstruct it exactly as shown.\n\nMulti-name ARG lines apparently work in buildah (clearly it was built with them: each single-name ARG entry at the bottom is from a previous failed build attempt; the successful build used multi-name ARGs… wait, no — the bottom single-ARG entries (46–59 minutes ago) might be from the same Containerfile: note the timestamps: \"ARG ROCM_WHL\" etc. at 59 minutes ago, \"ARG ROCM_DEVEL_PIN ROCM_WHL TORCHVISION_PIN TORCH_PIN TRITON_PIN\" at 46 minutes ago, and the 8-name one at 20 minutes ago. These look like different build attempts each accumulating a layer. Hmm, actually no: the single-name ARGs at 59m are probably from attempt 1; the 6-name at 46m from attempt 2; the 8-name + 10-name ones at 20 minutes ago from the successful build. But all appear in the final image's history chain — because the final image was built from the cached layers of previous attempts! Buildah's cache reuse: attempt N reuses layers from attempt N-1 if instructions match. The history chain of the final image: ubuntu base → ARG ROCM_WHL (59m)… wait, that would mean the final Containerfile had those ARGs. Hmm, but the 8-name ARG instruction entry is near the top (20m ago).\n\nLet me re-read the order (from bottom to top):\n1. Base ubuntu (ARG RELEASE, ARG LAUNCHPAD_BUILD_ARCH, LABEL, ADD, CMD)\n2. ARG ROCM_WHL (59m)\n3. ARG ROCM_WHL TORCH_PIN (59m)\n4. ARG ROCM_WHL TORCHVISION_PIN TORCH_PIN (59m)\n5. ARG ROCM_WHL TORCHVISION_PIN TORCH_PIN TRITON_PIN (59m)\n6. ARG ROCM_DEVEL_PIN ROCM_WHL TORCHVISION_PIN TORCH_PIN TRITON_PIN (46m)\n7. ARG DEVICE_PINS ROCM_DEVEL_PIN ROCM_WHL TORCHVISION_PIN TORCH_PIN TRITON_PIN (20m? shown as \"<missing>\" with no timestamp in the |8 group… the timestamp is omitted in the format output)\n8. ENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND\n9. RUN apt-get (20m ago)\n10. RUN python3.12 -m venv (20m)\n11. ENV VIRTUAL_ENV...\n12. RUN pip setuptools (20m)\n13. RUN pip torch set (18m)\n14. ARG DEVICE_PINS ... TRITON_PIN (redeclared)\n15. ARG DEVICE_PINS ... VLLM_REPO (8 names + VLLM_REPO? The entry shows \"ARG DEVICE_PINS ROCM_DEVEL_PIN ROCM_WHL TORCHVISION_PIN TORCH_PIN TRITON_PIN VLLM_BRANCH VLLM_REPO\" twice)\n16. ENV VLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=rocm PYTORCH_ROCM_ARCH=gfx1151\n17. RUN clone (17m)\n18. RUN pip install -e (7m)\n19. COPY rootfs (6m)\n20. RUN build-info python (6m)\n21. RUN cat build-info (6m)\n22. WORKDIR /opt/vllm\n23. CMD bash\n\nWait, but if the Containerfile has one ARG instruction per name, entries 2-7 show increasing accumulation: that's consistent with a single line like `ARG ROCM_WHL=... TORCH_PIN=...` getting appended to? No — entries 2-6 have different names listed each time, cumulative: ROCM_WHL → +TORCH_PIN → +TORCHVISION_PIN → +TRITON_PIN → +ROCM_DEVEL_PIN → +DEVICE_PINS. This is exactly the same as having separate `ARG name=value` lines added one by one across attempts, with each attempt's Containerfile being a prefix extension! Attempt 1 had ARG ROCM_WHL; attempt 2 added TORCH_PIN; and so on. But all of them are in the final chain… because each subsequent build reused the cache and the history contains the old layers. But wait, if attempt 3's Containerfile had 3 ARGs and attempt 6 had 6, they'd produce different layers (different instruction text); the final image's chain should contain only one path. The fact that all six are stacked means the final Containerfile had six separate ARG instructions?? But then it should show 6 separate \"ARG X\" entries each with one name… and it does — entries 2-7 each show cumulative names? No, the CREATED BY for an ARG instruction shows the ARG instruction text itself. Entry 2: \"ARG ROCM_WHL\" — instruction text was \"ARG ROCM_WHL\". Entry 3: \"ARG ROCM_WHL TORCH_PIN\" — instruction text \"ARG ROCM_WHL TORCH_PIN\"?? That's not valid Docker… unless buildah allows multi-name ARG? Actually, does buildah allow `ARG name1 name2`? Hmm.\n\nHmm, wait, maybe I'm misreading: entries 2-6 are from different attempts with different instruction texts, and what we're seeing is the history of a single chain where buildah replaced them? No, history is linear.\n\nLet me think again: entries 2-7 are a single linear chain: \"ARG ROCM_WHL\", then \"ARG ROCM_WHL TORCH_PIN\", then \"ARG ROCM_WHL TORCHVISION_PIN TORCH_PIN\", then… each instruction re-declares all previous names + one more. This is weird, but plausible as iterative authoring where the agent kept rewriting the ARG line to add one pin, and buildah cached each as a separate layer because the parent chain matched? For attempt k to reuse attempt k-1's layers, the prefix instructions must match. If the ARG line changed, cache miss at that layer… but since everything below is cheap (base image), it just re-executes from there.\n\nAnd the final build (20 minutes ago) continued from entry 7 (ARG DEVICE_PINS ... 6 names, timestamp around the apt-get layer) — so the final Containerfile's ARG section is exactly:\n```\nARG ROCM_WHL=...\nARG ROCM_WHL TORCH_PIN=...  (???)\n```\nNo wait. Cumulative entries would mean the Containerfile literally contains:\n```\nARG ROCM_WHL=...\nARG ROCM_WHL TORCH_PIN=...\nARG ROCM_WHL TORCHVISION_PIN TORCH_PIN=...\n```\nNonsense. Alternative: buildah shows ARG history entries as the accumulated set of ARGs in scope? That's more plausible! The CREATED BY of an ARG instruction shows \"ARG <all args declared up to this point>\". Hmm, but then entries 14/15 (two identical 8-name ARGs before ENV VLLM_TARGET_DEVICE) make sense as \"ARG VLLM_BRANCH ... ARG VLLM_REPO\" — two instructions, both showing the full accumulated set. And entry 7's |8 pip install is… ugh.\n\nOK, let me stop trying to fully decipher it. The real question is: what's buildah's cache key? buildah (containers/buildah) cache ID computation: for each instruction, the cache key includes the instruction type and its contents. For ARG/ENV/COPY, contents matter; for RUN, the command string + mount flags + … importantly, buildah's cache lookup uses a hash of the \"diff ID\" inputs that includes the command being run. If my Containerfile text differs at all, layers from that point on will be rebuilt.\n\nAlternative strategy: don't depend on exact text match. Instead, accept that podman build will reuse what it can and rebuild the rest. The expensive layer is `pip install -e /opt/vllm` (about 10+ minutes for compiling vllm? The previous full build was 64 minutes total). If cache misses at the ARG line, apt (about 2 min), venv (seconds), pip setuptools (about 10s), pip torch 8.5GB (wheel download — could be from cache directory? The wheels are in the local cache directory, and if we use --find-links as instructed… but the instruction uses --index-url ${ROCM_WHL} directly. Re-downloading 8.5GB over ethernet could take about 5-15 minutes), clone (a few minutes), pip install -e (the big one — vllm build with ninja; 17 minutes ago → the layer was created about 7 minutes ago; pip install -e took from 17 minutes ago to 7 minutes ago = 10 minutes).\n\nHmm. Risky but survivable? The full previous build was 64 minutes. If cache completely misses, worst case we're looking at about 40-50 minutes. The brief allows overnight iteration, but I'm an interactive agent. A re-run of about 1 hour is acceptable but not preferable.\n\nBetter approach: get exact text match. What's the probability that ARG entry format matters? buildah's cache ID for RUN includes the full command + environment variables? If environment variables (the |8 VAR=values) are part of the cache key… the default values for ARGs show up. As long as I use identical names and default values, no problem.\n\nFor the cumulative ARG entries: since they appear as separate layers in the chain, the Containerfile that built the final image must have contained all of these instructions: \"ARG ROCM_WHL=…\", \"ARG ROCM_WHL TORCH_PIN=…\" — wait, that can't be in a valid Dockerfile… unless buildah treats extra words as… hmm, let me test: create a trivial Containerfile `FROM ubuntu:24.04` + `ARG A=1 B=2` and see what history shows. This is cheap and will resolve the interpretation of multi-name ARG.\n\nActually, simpler: test whether a reconstruction gets cache hits. Plan:\n\n1. Extract the rootfs layer contents from the image to reproduce container/rootfs/ exactly.\n2. Reconstruct the Containerfile with the exact instruction text from history (best guess, with the multi-name ARGs as shown).\n3. Write build.sh per the spec: packaging tests first (tests/test_repo_invariants.py), podman build -t flashnext:dev, then extract build-info from the image to write results/receipts/build.json containing {step, status, ts, data:{torch, triton, fork_commit, image_digest}}.\n4. Run build.sh — see if the layers hit the cache. If early layers miss, adapt (e.g., try alternative ARG layouts).\n5. Acceptance argv, commit.\n\nFirst, the rootfs extraction. COPY dir:e37e34… → /opt/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/. Extract from image layer 8708085ccb37."}}
